'''aërial''' (ā-ē′ri-ạl), ''a.'' [Formerly also ''aëreal'', < L. ''aërius'' (= Gr. ἀέριος), also ''aëreus'', pertaining to the air, < ''aër'', < Gr. ἀήρ: see ''air''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Belonging or pertaining to the air or atmosphere; inhabiting or frequenting the air; existing or happening in the air; produced by or in the air: as, ''aërial'' regions; ''aërial'' perspective; ''aërial'' songsters; ''aërial'' ascents.
{{smaller block|<poem>Even till we make the main, and the ''aërial'' blue,
An indistinct regard.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, ii. 1.}}
<poem>''Aërial'' honey and ambrosial dews.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Virgil’s Georgics.}}}}

